Sval: browser-chrome test windows keep their own console

The mochitest harness replaced every browser window's console with a
Console.sys.mjs ConsoleAPI. DevTools' DocumentEventsListener.hasNativeConsoleAPI
then finds a non-native console on the window the Browser Console targets,
and WebConsoleUI.handleNavigated logs "The Web Console logging API ... has
been disabled by a script on this page" each time the Browser Console opens
in a test. The replacement is the harness's own, so only the harness can
stop it. The test profile sets devtools.console.stdout.chrome, which prints
the native console's calls to the log as the replacement did.

diff --git a/testing/mochitest/api.js b/testing/mochitest/api.js
index eafa0ee93a..46cb18d901 100644
--- a/testing/mochitest/api.js
+++ b/testing/mochitest/api.js
@@ -74,16 +74,11 @@ var WindowListener = {
   // browser-test.js is only loaded into the first window. Setup that
   // needs to happen in all navigator:browser windows should go here.
   setupWindow(win) {
+    // The window keeps its own console: DevTools reports any other object
+    // there as a page script disabling the logging API
+    // (DocumentEventsListener.hasNativeConsoleAPI), and the test profile's
+    // devtools.console.stdout.chrome already prints its calls to the log.
     win.nativeConsole = win.console;
-    let { ConsoleAPI } = ChromeUtils.importESModule(
-      "resource://gre/modules/Console.sys.mjs"
-    );
-    win.console = new ConsoleAPI();
-    // Add createInstance to the native wrapper so that it can be called from
-    // within windows.
-    win.console.createInstance = win.nativeConsole.createInstance.bind(
-      win.nativeConsole
-    );
   },
 
   tearDownWindow(win) {
